\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let $\mathbb D=\{z\in\mathbb C:|z|<1\}$ and $\mathbb T=\partial\mathbb D$. Call an analytic function $f$ annular if there are Jordan curves $J_n\subset\mathbb D$ such that $J_n$ lies in the bounded component of $\mathbb C\setminus J_{n+1}$, for every $\varepsilon>0$ all sufficiently late $J_n$ lie in $\{1-\varepsilon<|z|<1\}$, and $\min_{J_n}|f|\to\infty$. For $a\in\mathbb C$, let \[Z_{\partial}(f-a)=\{\zeta\in\mathbb T:\exists\text{ distinct }z_j\in\mathbb D,\ z_j\to\zeta,\ f(z_j)=a\},\quad S(f)=\{a\in\mathbb C:Z_{\partial}(f-a)\ne\mathbb T\}.\] This is the boundary accumulation set of the zeros, not the zero set itself.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Does there exist an annular $f$ for which $S(f)$ is countably infinite, that is, $|S(f)|=\aleph_0$? The update’s countability result for singular values of a strongly annular function concerns a different specified set. \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Can an annular analytic function have exactly countably infinitely many values whose preimages fail to accumulate at every boundary point?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 2305050 (AMR-022-5050). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Walter K. Hayman and Edward F. Lingham, \emph{Research Problems in Function Theory} (2018), arXiv:1809.07200. Problem 5.50, complete original question, all following updates, and the relevant chapter definitions. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}.
\end{description}
